(   [Problem Statement]: Write an ANSI C program to check whether a given integer is a Palindrome Number by reversing its digits.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Palindrome Number Verification Program:) Tj
T*
(   1. Algorithmic Logic:) Tj
T*
(   An integer is a palindrome if it reads identically forwards and backwards \(e.g., 12321\).) Tj
T*
(   - Negative numbers are not palindromes due to the leading minus sign.) Tj
T*
(   - Algorithm: Extract rightmost digits sequentially using modulo 10 \(n % 10\), append them to a) Tj
T*
(   reversed accumulator variable \(reversed = reversed * 10 + digit\), and divide n by 10 \(n /= 10\)) Tj
T*
(   until n becomes 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Compare reversed accumulator with preserved original number.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Complete ANSI C Source Code:) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdbool.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   bool is_palindrome_number\(int num\) {) Tj
T*
(   if \(num < 0\) return false; /* Negative numbers cannot be palindrome */) Tj
T*
(   if \(num >= 0 && num < 10\) return true; /* Single digits are trivial palindromes */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int original = num;) Tj
T*
(   long long reversed = 0;) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   while \(num > 0\) {) Tj
T*
(   int digit = num % 10;) Tj
T*
(   reversed = \(reversed * 10\) + digit;) Tj
T*
(   num /= 10;) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   return \(reversed == \(long long\)original\);) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
