(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the mechanics and application of the comma operator inside a for loop header for multiple initializations and updates.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Comma Operator in Multi-Variable for Loops:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem:) Tj
T*
(   A for loop header permits only three expressions separated by semicolons:) Tj
T*
(   for \(expression1; expression2; expression3\)) Tj
T*
(   If an algorithm requires manipulating two or more loop index counters simultaneously \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   reversing an array, matrix diagonal traversal\), standard syntax allows only one expression per) Tj
T*
(   slot.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Comma Operator Solution:) Tj
T*
(   The comma operator \(,\) allows combining multiple distinct expressions into a single expression.) Tj
T*
(   - Evaluates left expression, then right expression.) Tj
T*
(   - Creates a sequence point between evaluations.) Tj
T*
(   - Enables multiple variable initializations in expression1 and multiple variable modifications) Tj
T*
(   in expression3.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Demonstrative Code \(Two-Pointer In-Place Array Reversal\):) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   void print_array\(const int *arr, int n\) {) Tj
T*
(   for \(int i = 0; i < n; i++\) printf\("%d ", arr[i]\);) Tj
T*
(   printf\("\\n"\);) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int main\(void\) {) Tj
T*
(   int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80};) Tj
T*
