(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the concept of an infinite loop in C, explaining intentional daemon loops, termination conditions, and accidental causes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Infinite Loops in C: Design, Applications, and Bugs:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition:) Tj
T*
(   A loop construct whose termination condition never evaluates to false \(0\), executing) Tj
T*
(   indefinitely unless interrupted by a break, return, exit\(\), or external OS signal.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Standard Syntactical Forms:) Tj
T*
(   - Form 1: while \(1\) { ... }) Tj
T*
(   - Form 2: for \(;;\) { ... } [Canonical C idiom: empty condition defaults to true]) Tj
T*
(   - Form 3: do { ... } while \(1\);) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Intentional Legitimate Use-Cases:) Tj
T*
(   - Operating System Kernels: The idle process or main scheduling loop.) Tj
T*
(   - Embedded Systems / Microcontrollers: The main firmware superloop:) Tj
T*
(   void main\(\) { setup\(\); while\(1\) { loop\(\); } }) Tj
T*
(   - Event-Driven Server Daemons: Listening indefinitely for incoming TCP socket connections.) Tj
T*
(   - Graceful Exit via Break: Infinite loop containing internal conditional checks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Accidental Causes \(Bugs\):) Tj
T*
(   - Unintentional Semicolon: while \(i < 10\); { i++; } -> Empty statement loops forever!) Tj
T*
(   - Missing Increment: while \(i < 10\) { printf\("%d\\n", i\); } -> i never advances.) Tj
T*
(   - Overflow Wraparound: for \(unsigned char i = 0; i <= 255; i++\) -> 255 + 1 wraps to 0, never) Tj
T*
(   exceeding 255!) Tj
T*
(   - Floating-Point Imprecision: for \(float f = 0.0f; f != 1.0f; f += 0.1f\) -> f never exactly) Tj
T*
(   equals 1.0f due to binary rounding!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
