(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the format string vulnerability in C, showing how user-controlled format strings cause arbitrary memory disclosure and writes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Format String Vulnerability: Mechanics and Prevention:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Root Cause:) Tj
T*
(   Functions in the printf\(\) family expect the format string argument to dictate the number, type,) Tj
T*
(   and stack offsets of trailing arguments:) Tj
T*
(   int printf\(const char *format, ...\);) Tj
T*
(   If an application passes untrusted user input directly as the format argument:) Tj
T*
(   char user_buf[100];) Tj
T*
(   fgets\(user_buf, sizeof\(user_buf\), stdin\);) Tj
T*
(   printf\(user_buf\); /* FATAL SECURITY VULNERABILITY! */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Exploitation Vectors:) Tj
T*
(   - Memory Disclosure \(Information Leak\):) Tj
T*
(   If user inputs "%x %x %x %x", printf pops values off the stack corresponding to missing) Tj
T*
(   arguments, printing secret cryptographic keys, return addresses, and stack canaries!) Tj
T*
(   - Arbitrary Memory Read:) Tj
T*
(   Supplying "%s" causes printf to treat values on stack as memory addresses and dereference them,) Tj
T*
(   printing contents of arbitrary memory or causing SIGSEGV.) Tj
T*
(   - Arbitrary Memory Overwrite \(%n specifier\):) Tj
T*
(   The %n specifier writes the number of characters printed so far into a target integer pointer.) Tj
T*
(   Attackers craft format strings to overwrite function pointers and hijack execution!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Secure Defensive Coding Rules:) Tj
T*
(   - NEVER pass raw user strings as the format string.) Tj
T*
(   - ALWAYS provide an explicit format specifier:) Tj
T*
(   printf\("%s", user_buf\); /* Immune to format string attacks */) Tj
T*
(   fputs\(user_buf, stdout\); /* Even faster and fully secure */) Tj
T*
() Tj
